1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,905
Start with a photo on your phone. To send it
somewhere

2
00:00:04,905 --> 00:00:09,810
else, the phone must turn that photo into a
pattern another

3
00:00:09,810 --> 00:00:14,714
device can read. A cable carries that changing
pattern between places.

4
00:00:14,714 --> 00:00:19,619
Some cables use electrical signals. This thin
glass strand uses light.

5
00:00:19,619 --> 00:00:24,524
It is an optical fiber. Watch one light pulse
travel through

6
00:00:24,524 --> 00:00:29,429
it. That is our starting point: something
changes at one end,

7
00:00:29,429 --> 00:00:34,333
and the other end detects the change.

8
00:00:34,333 --> 00:00:39,429
How can light stand for a photo? Start much
smaller: a

9
00:00:39,429 --> 00:00:44,524
yes or a no. We can agree that a bright pulse

10
00:00:44,524 --> 00:00:49,619
means one and a dark time means zero. Each
choice is

11
00:00:49,619 --> 00:00:54,714
called a bit. Put many bits together, and
their pattern can

12
00:00:54,714 --> 00:00:59,810
represent text, sound, or a picture. Our
blinking light is a

13
00:00:59,810 --> 00:01:04,905
simple teaching example. Real equipment can
use more detailed patterns, but

14
00:01:04,905 --> 00:01:10,000
the basic job stays the same: carry
information using changes.

15
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,029
Now look closer at the light itself. We draw
it as

16
00:01:15,029 --> 00:01:20,057
a wave, with repeating peaks. This curve shows
a repeating light

17
00:01:20,057 --> 00:01:25,086
property; the beam does not wiggle through the
cable like a

18
00:01:25,086 --> 00:01:30,114
snake. The distance from one peak to the next
is its

19
00:01:30,114 --> 00:01:35,143
wavelength. Wide spacing means a longer
wavelength. Close spacing means a

20
00:01:35,143 --> 00:01:40,171
shorter one. That spacing gives us a way to
tell different

21
00:01:40,171 --> 00:01:45,200
light signals apart, even when they share a
path.

22
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:50,167
Bring in four senders. Each has its own bits
to send:

23
00:01:50,167 --> 00:01:55,133
four different conversations. So far, our
easiest drawing gives every conversation

24
00:01:55,133 --> 00:02:00,100
its own path. Blue can carry one pattern while
green carries

25
00:02:00,100 --> 00:02:05,067
another. Neither needs to copy the other. We
call each separately

26
00:02:05,067 --> 00:02:10,033
usable signal path a channel. Four channels,
four independent messages. But

27
00:02:10,033 --> 00:02:15,000
building a separate long cable for every
conversation sounds expensive. Can

28
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:19,967
these four channels share just one glass
strand?

29
00:02:19,967 --> 00:02:25,124
Before solving that, try a bad idea. Put two
changing signals

30
00:02:25,124 --> 00:02:30,281
into the same wavelength region and ask the
receiver to separate

31
00:02:30,281 --> 00:02:35,438
them using wavelength alone. There is no
useful difference for that

32
00:02:35,438 --> 00:02:40,595
filter to select. Our colored boxes overlap to
show the problem.

33
00:02:40,595 --> 00:02:45,752
Simply mixing more light is not enough. We
need a recognizable

34
00:02:45,752 --> 00:02:50,910
difference that the receiving equipment can
use. Think of labels on

35
00:02:50,910 --> 00:02:56,067
luggage: sharing a conveyor works better when
the bags remain identifiable.

36
00:02:56,067 --> 00:03:00,971
Here is the useful difference. Give each
channel a different wavelength.

37
00:03:00,971 --> 00:03:05,876
Our four colors are labels for those different
spacings. They do

38
00:03:05,876 --> 00:03:10,781
not mean four visible colored beams;
communication fibers commonly carry infrared

39
00:03:10,781 --> 00:03:15,686
light that our eyes cannot see. Each channel
also needs some

40
00:03:15,686 --> 00:03:20,590
space around its chosen wavelength because its
information changes over time.

41
00:03:20,590 --> 00:03:25,495
Spread the channels apart, leaving suitable
room for the signals and

42
00:03:25,495 --> 00:03:30,400
for equipment that will separate them later.

43
00:03:30,400 --> 00:03:35,367
At the sending end, an optical device brings
the separate channels

44
00:03:35,367 --> 00:03:40,333
onto one shared path. It is called a
multiplexer. The name

45
00:03:40,333 --> 00:03:45,300
is longer than the idea: many inputs, one
shared output. Follow

46
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:50,267
blue into the box, then green, yellow, and
purple. Each still

47
00:03:50,267 --> 00:03:55,233
carries its own changing information. The
device combines their light for

48
00:03:55,233 --> 00:04:00,200
the journey. It does not merge the four
messages into a

49
00:04:00,200 --> 00:04:05,167
new sentence or make their bit patterns
identical.

50
00:04:05,167 --> 00:04:10,195
Now watch the middle of the link. The four
wavelength channels

51
00:04:10,195 --> 00:04:15,224
travel through the same fiber at the same
time. Our small

52
00:04:15,224 --> 00:04:20,252
vertical gaps are only there so you can follow
the colors

53
00:04:20,252 --> 00:04:25,281
on screen; there are no four little pipes
inside the glass.

54
00:04:25,281 --> 00:04:30,310
This sharing method is wavelength division
multiplexing, or WDM. The words

55
00:04:30,310 --> 00:04:35,338
now describe something we have already seen:
divide the available wavelengths

56
00:04:35,338 --> 00:04:40,367
into channels, then let those channels share a
fiber.

57
00:04:40,367 --> 00:04:45,524
Sharing only helps if we can recover the
separate information. At

58
00:04:45,524 --> 00:04:50,681
the far end, another optical device sorts
different wavelength regions toward

59
00:04:50,681 --> 00:04:55,838
different outputs. It is called a
demultiplexer: the separating partner of

60
00:04:55,838 --> 00:05:00,995
the combining box. Follow blue to its matching
output. Green goes

61
00:05:00,995 --> 00:05:06,152
to another. The receivers can now detect
changes in their selected

62
00:05:06,152 --> 00:05:11,310
channels. Four messages entered the system,
traveled together, and came out

63
00:05:11,310 --> 00:05:16,467
as four separately readable streams. The
wavelength difference survived the journey.

64
00:05:16,467 --> 00:05:21,033
Let us follow only the green channel. Dim the
others so

65
00:05:21,033 --> 00:05:25,600
your eye can stay with one bit pattern. Green
enters, travels

66
00:05:25,600 --> 00:05:30,167
alongside the other light, and reaches the
green output. Its receiver

67
00:05:30,167 --> 00:05:34,733
reads those changes. It does not need the blue
message to

68
00:05:34,733 --> 00:05:39,300
stop talking first. This is the central trick:
the shared fiber

69
00:05:39,300 --> 00:05:43,867
carries several distinguishable channels at
once. Our labels track their identities;

70
00:05:43,867 --> 00:05:48,433
the actual separation is performed by optical
equipment designed for their

71
00:05:48,433 --> 00:05:53,000
wavelengths.

72
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:58,157
Now the numbers have a picture to attach to. A
bit

73
00:05:58,157 --> 00:06:03,314
rate tells us how many bits a channel carries
each second.

74
00:06:03,314 --> 00:06:08,471
Suppose each of our four channels carries one
hundred billion bits

75
00:06:08,471 --> 00:06:13,629
per second. We shorten that to one hundred
gigabits per second,

76
00:06:13,629 --> 00:06:18,786
written Gb/s. Add the channels one at a time:
one hundred,

77
00:06:18,786 --> 00:06:23,943
two hundred, three hundred, four hundred. Four
equal channels provide four

78
00:06:23,943 --> 00:06:29,100
hundred gigabits per second of combined raw
capacity in this example.

79
00:06:29,100 --> 00:06:33,779
There is one small trap in that impressive
number. File sizes

80
00:06:33,779 --> 00:06:38,458
often use bytes, while link rates often use
bits. A byte

81
00:06:38,458 --> 00:06:43,138
is a group of eight bits. Watch eight little
boxes become

82
00:06:43,138 --> 00:06:47,817
one group. To turn four hundred gigabits per
second into gigabytes

83
00:06:47,817 --> 00:06:52,496
per second, divide by eight. The answer is
fifty. That is

84
00:06:52,496 --> 00:06:57,175
a raw capacity conversion, not a promise that
a download reaches

85
00:06:57,175 --> 00:07:01,854
fifty gigabytes every second. The rest of the
system also takes

86
00:07:01,854 --> 00:07:06,533
time and capacity.

87
00:07:06,533 --> 00:07:11,100
If four channels help, why not squeeze in
endlessly more? Look

88
00:07:11,100 --> 00:07:15,667
at the spaces between the channels. The
equipment has a usable

89
00:07:15,667 --> 00:07:20,233
range, and the signals need enough room to
remain distinguishable. Add

90
00:07:20,233 --> 00:07:24,800
too many into the same space and separation
becomes harder. Noise

91
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:29,367
and other physical effects matter too.
Engineers must match the fiber,

92
00:07:29,367 --> 00:07:33,933
light sources, amplifiers, and receivers. More
channels can increase capacity, but

93
00:07:33,933 --> 00:07:38,500
the drawing cannot create unlimited room that
real equipment does not

94
00:07:38,500 --> 00:07:43,067
have.

95
00:07:43,067 --> 00:07:47,633
Our channels do not have to carry equal
amounts. Keep blue

96
00:07:47,633 --> 00:07:52,200
and green at one hundred gigabits per second,
but let yellow

97
00:07:52,200 --> 00:07:56,767
and purple carry fifty each. Add what each
contributes: one hundred

98
00:07:56,767 --> 00:08:01,333
plus one hundred plus fifty plus fifty gives
three hundred. The

99
00:08:01,333 --> 00:08:05,900
rule is still simple addition of the usable
channel rates in

100
00:08:05,900 --> 00:08:10,467
our example. Counting colors alone is not
enough. Four channels can

101
00:08:10,467 --> 00:08:15,033
have different total capacity depending on how
much information each channel

102
00:08:15,033 --> 00:08:19,600
carries.

103
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:24,225
Try a quick prediction. Return to four
channels carrying one hundred

104
00:08:24,225 --> 00:08:28,850
gigabits per second each. Now switch off the
green transmitter while

105
00:08:28,850 --> 00:08:33,475
keeping the rest of our model unchanged. Does
all the light

106
00:08:33,475 --> 00:08:38,100
stop? No. The other three channels still
travel. The combined raw

107
00:08:38,100 --> 00:08:42,725
rate drops from four hundred to three hundred
gigabits per second.

108
00:08:42,725 --> 00:08:47,350
Green has gone quiet; it has not taken the
whole fiber

109
00:08:47,350 --> 00:08:51,975
with it. Distinct channels give us a useful
way to understand

110
00:08:51,975 --> 00:08:56,600
the result.

111
00:08:56,600 --> 00:09:01,167
We can now rebuild the whole idea from the
beginning. A

112
00:09:01,167 --> 00:09:05,733
message becomes a changing signal. Different
wavelength channels give several signals

113
00:09:05,733 --> 00:09:10,300
identities the equipment can distinguish. One
device combines them, one glass

114
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:14,867
strand carries them, and another device
separates them. Adding the channel

115
00:09:14,867 --> 00:09:19,433
rates tells us the combined raw capacity. That
is WDM. The

116
00:09:19,433 --> 00:09:24,000
fiber did not become four separate cables. We
learned how to

117
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:28,567
let several conversations share the same
physical path while keeping them

118
00:09:28,567 --> 00:09:33,133
readable.
